  "description": {
   "content": [
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "Fedora Repository 3.1, Object Profile HTML presentation. E.g. ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "marks": [
        {
         "attrs": {
          "href": "http://localhost:8080/fedora/get/demo:1"
         },
         "type": "link"
        }
       ],
       "text": "http://localhost:8080/fedora/get/demo:1",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "text": ".",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "All key elements appear in blue, both true links and \"header\" elements. See screenshot.",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "The color blue is universally recognized on the Web as the color for clickable links. Other elements drawn in blue tend to appear to be clickable when in reality they aren't. ",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "It is impossible to know what is a link (and what isn't) by mere examination. One must mouse over a blue element to decide if it's a link. Confusing and needlessly so. Can a different color be used for text elements and links be left to their native, default color?",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "(Granted, links may also be underlined. But blue links is as a de facto, established Web \"standard\".)",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "The lack of CSS makes this difficult to change, unfortunately. ",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    }
   ],
